4.4.2.m:
"O divine waters, rich in honey, mingle, winning great ruling power for the kṣatriya," [he says];
for these are the winners of ruling power.
"Sit down, unassailed, full of sap, bestowing great splendor on the kṣatriya," [he says];
for these are the sustainers of ruling power.
"You are uncrushed," he says;
he has indeed made the realm uncrushed.
"Born of ascetic heat," he says;
for the realm is born of ascetic heat.
"The gift of Soma," he says;
for this is the gift of Soma.
"Pure ones, I purify you with the pure; gleaming ones, I purify you with the gleaming," he says;
life indeed is gold;
he has made them prolongers of life;
splendor indeed is gold;
he has made them bestowers of splendor.
"May the god Savitṛ purify you with an unslit strainer," he says;
this indeed is the unslit strainer, namely the rays of the sun;
with an unslit strainer indeed he purifies them.
"Svāhā, belonging to the royal consecration!" he says;
for these belong to the royal consecration.
"Feasting together, splendid, together with invigorating drink," he divides [the water] with a [verse] to Varuṇa;
for the waters belong to Varuṇa,
by his own deity.
"O Rudra, whatever name of yours extends beyond the mountains," he says;
thereby he appeases Rudra away from him.
"In that it is offered;
it is sacrificed to Yama, svāhā!" he says;
Yama indeed is death;
thereby he appeases death away from him.
"May Soma, Indra, Varuṇa, Mitra, Agni—these gods who uphold cosmic order—uphold cosmic order," he says;
these gods who uphold cosmic order are these breathings;
if they were to consecrate him without announcing him to them, they would be excluders of him;
now, in that they consecrate him having announced him to them, thus they do not exclude him.
At the tip, at the edge of the leaf [of the parṇa tree], they conversed about the brahman;
that indeed was Suśravas, who heard it;
with a vessel of parṇa wood he consecrates [him];
the Brahman-priest consecrates [him];
with brahman-splendor indeed he consecrates him;
moreover, the Brahman-priest is the brahman;
by the brahman indeed the Brahman-priest consecrates him.
With a vessel of aśvattha wood a vaiśya [consecrates him];
the heroic power of the people went away;
it entered the aśvattha tree;
it trembled with that heroic power;
thereby indeed he obtains the heroic power of the people.
With a vessel of udumbara wood a rival [consecrates him];
udumbara trees are invigorating drink;
the sacrificer thereby deprives the rival of invigorating drink, of food.
He who is a stranger, an ally, [consecrates] with a vessel of nyagrodha wood;
by an ally indeed ruling power is spread out;
the nyagrodha tree has downward roots;
by an ally indeed he spreads out ruling power,
for firmness, for not becoming loose.

4.4.3.m:
"You are the womb of ruling power, / you are the amnion of ruling power, / you are the navel of ruling power," [he says].
When Indra was born, that was indeed his womb which is the tārpya garment;
the amnion was the white garment;
the navel was the turban.
He was born from a barren cow that had calved.
The empty udder had four teats;
the first-milk was nīvāra rice.
Therefore the sacrificial cake is made of nīvāra rice.
He anoints fifty [pebbles] on one axle, fifty-one on the other.
A man has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundredfold heroic powers.
He obtains lifespan and heroic power.
He touches water.
Water indeed is immortality.
By immortality he causes him to take hold.
"Announced is Agni the house-lord," [he says];
he announces him to the Gārhapatya fire.
"Announced is Indra of exalted fame," [he says];
Indra is virile power;
he announces him to virile power.
"Announced are Mitra and Varuṇa of steadfast vows," [he says];
Mitra and Varuṇa are day and night;
he announces him to day and night.
"Announced are Heaven and Earth, increasers of order," [he says];
he announces him to Heaven and Earth.
"Announced is Pūṣan the all-possessing," [he says];
Pūṣan is cattle;
he announces him to cattle.
"Announced is the goddess Aditi," [he says];
these creatures are the Ādityas;
he announces him to them.
"Announced is this one, so-and-so, son of so-and-so, son of such-and-such mother, in such-and-such community," [he says];
he announces him to the community.
Therefore he is dear to the community;
for he announces him to the community.
"This is your king, O people," [he says];
he announces him to the people.
"Soma is the king of us Brahmins," [he says, and] sets aside the priesthood.
He announces him to all other beings,
[and] sets aside the priesthood.
Therefore they say:
"The priesthood is set aside;"
for he sets aside the priesthood.
"You are Indra's thunderbolt, slayer of Vṛtra; / with you may this one slay Vṛtra," [he says, and] gives the bow.
The bow is a thunderbolt;
the rājanya is a thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he makes the thunderbolt prosper.
"You are crushers of enemies," [he says, giving] quivers.
The quivers are the end;
the rājanya is the end;
with the end he makes the end prosper.
He gives three [arrows].
Three indeed are the arrows: celestial, earthly, and of the sea;
may he keep them away from him.
"Protect to the front;
protect to the rear;
protect across;
protect along;
protect upward."
"Protect him from the quarters!" [he says];
they protect him from the quarters.
"You are Mitra," [he says,] addressing this one;
for this is Mitra's form.
"You are Varuṇa," [he says,] addressing that one;
for that is Varuṇa's form.
With "Golden-hued, at the break of dawns," an [invocation] containing [the word] "up", he should address him.
He sprinkles him while standing with arms raised;
the chant is the Bṛhat [Sāman];
he connects him with the heavenly world.

4.4.4.m:
He causes him to step out toward the quarters.
With the thunderbolt he conquers these quarters.
Therefore all the quarters are conquered by him toward whichever one he turns around.
The quarters are the heavenly world;
these quarters belong to these deities;
according to the deities he goes from these quarters to the heavenly world.
"You are Soma's brightness; possessor of brightness, / may my brightness become like yours," [he says, and] unrolls the tiger skin.
One brightness is in Soma,
one in the tiger,
one in the serpent;
he wins them.
"The head of Namuci is thrown down," [he says, and] throws lead at a eunuch.
Namuci's head is evil;
he throws evil away from him, ascending to the heavenly world.
"The biting serpents are driven away," [he says, giving] copper to a barber;
thereby he averts the biting serpents from him.
Therefore he has no sorcery against him;
for however many deaths there are, he averts them from him.
"Protect from death," [he says, and] pushes under a silver plate from below.
The tiger is the form of death;
gold is immortality;
with immortality he hides death away.
"Protect from the sky-fire," [he says, and] places a golden plate on top;
he keeps him away from the divine lightning bolt.
This [earth] is the silver one;
that [heaven] is the golden one;
enclosing him with these two,
gold is lifespan;
[waters] imparting lifespan flow over him;
gold is splendor;
[waters] imparting splendor flow over him.
When Varuṇa was being sprinkled, the waters knocked out his virile power and heroic power.
In that he puts the plate in between,
[it is] for the non-destruction of virile power and heroic power.
It weighs a hundred mānas, has a hundred syllables.
A man has a lifespan of a hundred [years], a hundredfold heroic powers;
he obtains lifespan and heroic power.

4.4.5.m:
Now these Pārtha [oblations]:
the Pārtha [oblations] are the year;
he enters into the year from the middle;
therefore this one is hard to push away,
for he enters into the year from the middle.
These two are the mountains of the rājasūya:
the [libation] to Bṛhaspati of some is the last, that to Indra of some is the first.
Bṛhaspati is heroic power;
Indra is heroic power;
he unites him with heroic power.
These are the names of Āditya;
these creatures belong to Āditya;
for them indeed he is consecrated.
"I consecrate you with the brightness of Soma, with the fiery energy of Agni, with the virile power of Indra, with the mental power of all the gods," [he says];
with these virile powers, with these deities he consecrates him.
"You are the womb of Indra; / beget!" [he says];
from here indeed Indra was born;
from his own womb he begets him.
"Protect beyond the days," he says,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
"They have turned around downwards, upwards," [he says and] wipes up;
when he is consecrated, virile power, heroic power goes away from him;
that very thing he wipes up;
with it he prospers him.
With a Triṣṭubh verse addressed to Indra the Brahman priest follows.
The nobleman belongs to Indra and has the Triṣṭubh meter;
with his own meter he follows him.
Moreover, the Brahman priest is the Prajāpati of the sacrifice;
Indra was consecrated from Prajāpati;
from Prajāpati he is consecrated.
"You are Indra's thunderbolt, winning booty; / through you may this one win booty," [he says and] brings down the chariot,
with his own deity.
"I yoke you at the impulse of Mitra and Varuṇa, the two directors," [he says and] yokes [it],
with his own deity.
"You are the stride of Viṣṇu, slayer of the rival," [he says and] mounts the chariot.
From here indeed Viṣṇu conquered these worlds;
following the conquest of Viṣṇu he conquers these worlds;
he thrusts the rival forth from these worlds.
"Conquer at the impulse of the Maruts," [he says];
having become a host of the Maruts, he conquers;
he defeats the nobleman.
He has stepped to the end;
then no one remains undefeated for him.
At him he shoots that arrow;
then his shooting was not in vain.
Having said, "Mind is obtained together with virile power," he turns around;
having gone to the end, he turns around,
for the non-injury of himself.
"This is the thunderbolt, best winner of booty; with it may our son win booty," [he says and] hands the bow and the end of the bow to his wife;
this indeed is the wife's taking hold of the sacrifice;
they are together in the heavenly world.

4.4.6.m:
"So much you are; / lifespan you are; / bestow lifespan on me," [he says];
he bestows lifespan on him.
"You are joined; / splendor you are; / bestow splendor on me," [he says];
he bestows splendor on him.
"Sap you are; / bestow sap in me," [he says].
This [earth] is the silver [shoe];
that [sky] is the golden [shoe];
these two having touched,
for the udumbara is sap,
he places sap in the middle of himself and of creatures.
"You are Mitra," [he says and] takes down this one;
for this is the form of Mitra.
"You are Varuṇa," [he says and takes down] this one;
for this is the form of Varuṇa.
"You are the existent; / may I [be united] with all the gods," [he says and] brings his hands down toward the curd-mixture.
The curd-mixture is the existent;
he brings them down toward the existent.
There he gives those two pieces of gold;
that is the sacrificial fee.
He indeed injures animals when descending upon the earth;
"Homage to mother earth," [he says and] descends onto the boar-skin shoes;
he descends upon wild animals;
only the boar is able to endure this;
so the boar endures him.
"That royal power has been established as name; Varuṇa has consecrated his own body," [he says];
this is the Varuṇa-consecration;
he announces him to Varuṇa.
"Trita has brought old age down upon us," [he says];
he unites old age with him.
"Pleasant you are, comfortable to sit upon," [he says];
for it is pleasant, comfortable to sit upon.
"Sit on the pleasant, / sit on the comfortable to sit upon," [he says];
he seats him on what is comfortable to sit upon.
"Varuṇa of steadfast vow has sat down among the dwellings, / the very skillful, for universal rule," [he says];
this is the Varuṇa-consecration;
he has made him Varuṇa.
"To Agni svāhā! / To Soma svāhā! / To Indra's vigor svāhā! / To the strength of the Maruts svāhā!" [he says and] offers the chariot-unyoking [offering].
With a yajus they are yoked, with a yajus they are unyoked.
"The swan dwelling in the clear, the Vasu dwelling in the atmosphere," [he says];
he praises the chariot.
Together with the charioteer they place the chariot in the chariot-stand,
for totality, for impulse.
"O Brahman, you are the Brahman," [he says];
"What have I become? What have I become?" is what he says.
"You are Savitṛ, of true impulse," [he says];
"You are this, you are this," is what he says to him, whatever he becomes.
"This is the thunderbolt; with it subdue for me," [he says and] hands over the wooden sword.
The wooden sword is the thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he subdues the realm.
He hands it over from one to the next;
with it he goes, subduing the realm from one to the next;
therefore these, subdued by him from one to the next,
bring tribute.
With that wooden sword they make the gaming ground;
there they gamble for a four-year-old cow.
He consecrates toward hope;
the four-year-old cow is hope;
he has made hope for him.
Then, having drawn out a hundred and four dice, he says:
"Won for the king!"
There are four men:
the brāhmaṇa, the nobleman, the vaiśya, the śūdra;
from among them he makes him win out.
Then, handing over five dice, he says:
"This one has overcome the quarters."
These five quarters he hands over to him for food.
He gives field;
thereby he is supported in the field.
He chooses a wish;
that wish of his is fulfilled, whatever he desires.
He calls [him] by auspicious names;
"Lest what I uttered previously be in vain," [he thinks].
"So-and-so, son of so-and-so; of so-and-so, so-and-so is the son," [he says and] intertwines the two names,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.

4.4.7.m:
This sacrifice is the same for a year.
The Daśapeya is cut off when they go down to the final bath.
When they come making offerings with: "To the son of waters, svāhā! To the son of strength, svāhā! To Agni, lord of the house, svāhā!",
[it is] for the continuity of the sacrifice, for non-interruption.
The sacrifice vanished from the gods;
the gods sought it in the Saṃsṛps.
When there is one for Savitṛ,
impelled by Savitṛ they sought [it].
When there is one for Sarasvatī,
Sarasvatī is speech;
in speech they sought [it].
When there is one for Pūṣan,
Pūṣan is cattle;
in cattle they sought [it].
When there is one for Bṛhaspati,
Bṛhaspati is the sacred word;
in the sacred word they sought [it].
When there is one for Indra,
Indra is virile power;
in virile power they sought [it].
When there is one for Varuṇa,
in Varuṇa they sought [it].
When there is one for Tvaṣṭṛ,
Tvaṣṭṛ is the year;
in the year they sought [it].
When there is one for Agni,
Agni is all the deities;
in the deities they sought [it].
When there is one for Soma,
plants belong to Soma;
in plants they sought [it].
When there is one for Viṣṇu,
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
in the sacrifice they sought [it].
They found it in Viṣṇu, the tenth;
therefore this is the tenth;
for they found it in the tenth.
There are ten cups;
there are ten cup-Adhvaryus;
he counts out ten cups on both sides up to the tenth generation of men,
for success;
for this sacrifice is ten-fold successful.
They sought that sacrifice then;
they prospered.
Therefore he who sacrifices with this prospers.
Everything becomes seventeenfold;
Prajāpati is seventeenfold;
he reaches Prajāpati.
This is a discontinued sacrifice;
a discontinued sacrifice is obtained from the year;
having reached it from the year, he obtains it.
Indra killed Vṛtra;
these forms approached him:
this [earth] with bright forms, that [sky] with stars.
In the reflection of the stars, a lotus grows;
this is the form of royal power;
he puts on the form of royal power.
There are twelve lotuses;
twelve months are the year;
having reached the year, he obtains it.

4.4.8.m:
A gold ornament is for the Hotṛ;
the Hotṛ belongs to Agni;
[the dawn] does not shine out for him;
he causes it to shine out for him.
A garland is for the Udgātṛ;
the Udgātṛ belongs to Sūrya;
moreover, having reached yonder sun, he leads it up for him.
Dice are for the Adhvaryu;
for the Adhvaryu is like the twin.
A horse is for the Prastotṛ;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
the Prastotṛ belongs to Prajāpati;
and also because he snorts forward, as it were;
the Prastotṛ is forward, as it were.
A milch cow is for the Pratihartṛ;
for she takes toward, as it were;
the Pratihartṛ is toward, as it were.
A barren cow is for the Maitrāvaruṇa,
[with the thought]: "May he bring me under control."
A bull is for the Brāhmaṇācchaṃsin,
for possession of virile power.
A garment is for the Potṛ,
for purity.
A draft-ox is for the Neṣṭṛ, another for the Agnīdh,
just for yoking.
A single-horse load of barley is for the Acchāvāka;
for this office of the Acchāvāka is like a one-horse cart leading to heaven;
and the barley is for being free from Varuṇa.
At that time gifts are not given to the Sadasyas for this [sacrifice];
by that they are sought and pleased for him.
Twelve four-year-old pregnant cows are for the Brahman;
speech is a milch cow;
the mantra is an embryo;
he places the mantra in his speech;
he becomes one who is to be addressed.
They are about to become milch cows;
there are twelve milks;
he places them in him.
Therefore they say of him:
"A rich king is auspicious,"
for as much milk as there is, that is placed in him.

4.4.9.m:
The Śrāyantīya is the Brahman's sāman.
They transpose the Yajñāyajñīya into anuṣṭubh verses.
The Vāravantīya is the Agniṣṭoma-sāman.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
he was deprived of vigor and heroic power.
He saw this sāman;
by it he supported himself.
He who is consecrated by the Rājasūya is deprived of vigor and heroic power;
so by this very [sāman] he supports himself.
When the sacred word (brahman) differentiated itself in the beginning as ṛc, sāman, [and] yajus,
the essence of it is the Yajñāyajñīya.
When the Yajñāyajñīya is sung,
he places essence right into the sacred word.
He who is consecrated by the Rājasūya is deprived of speech;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
when the Yajñāyajñīya is in anuṣṭubh verses,
he places essence right into his speech.
He who is consecrated by the Rājasūya is deprived of faculty and heroic power;
the Vāravantīya is faculty and heroic power;
when the Vāravantīya is the Agniṣṭoma-sāman,
[it is] for the securing of faculty and heroic power.
They leaned on [it] with the Śrāyantīya;
they warded off with the Vāravantīya;
that is for the encompassing of his faculty and heroic power.
When he makes him step out in those directions there, then he ascends to the heavenly world.
If he were not to descend again to this world, he would either go beyond the people or run wild.
When there are these Aveṣṭis of the quarters,
he descends again to this world.
One desiring food should sacrifice.
These quarters have these deities;
according to the deity, from these quarters he secures proper food.
If a Brahmin should sacrifice, having placed [an offering] to Bṛhaspati in the center and having made offering after offering, he should baste it.
If a Vaiśya, [an offering] to the Viśve Devas.
If a Rājanya, [an offering] to Indra.
He establishes proper food in him in a single manner.
This sacrifice is the same for a year.
The sacrifice is cut off when it is concluded.
When he undertakes these Prayuj offerings, some in the evening, some in the morning—
two seasons are day and night;
those two he joins;
six are the others, six the others;
six are the seasons;
he joins the seasons;
both together are twelve;
twelve months are the year;
he joins the year.
Two draft-oxen are the sacrificial gift,
for the sake of yoking.
[There is an offering of] a dappled four-year-old cow, pregnant, for the Maruts;
the Maruts are the common people;
he makes him an embryo of the common people;
therefore he is dear to the common people,
for an embryo is dear.
[There is an offering of] a barren (?) pregnant cow for Aditi;
Aditi is this [earth];
he makes him an embryo of this [earth];
therefore he is hard to drive away,
for he is the embryo of this [earth].
Then these are the true messengers:
for impulsion, the one to Savitṛ.
The gods, going to victory over the Asuras, deposited truth in the two Aśvins and Pūṣan.
Sarasvatī is speech;
by speech he sends forth truth.
'I have been consecrated, I have become king,' [he says];
they place trust in him who has pressed [Soma].
For a year he offers the Agnihotra,
for continuity.

4.4.10.m:
At the beginning he brings forward the Agniṣṭoma, the Jyotiṣṭoma.
The Agniṣṭoma is the mouth of the sacrifice;
having grasped the mouth of the sacrifice, he steps into the consecration.
Then this Triṣṭoma:
its morning pressing is threefold (trivṛt), its midday pressing fifteenfold (pañcadaśa);
the threefold is brilliance,
the fifteenfold is lordship;
he places brilliance in his lordship.
Not even the seventeenfold is extended here;
Prajāpati is the seventeenfold;
the sacrifice is Prajāpati;
since the sacrifice is extended,
thereby the seventeenfold is not excluded.
The midday pressing is fifteenfold,
the third pressing twenty-one-fold;
the twenty-one-fold is the common people,
the fifteenfold is lordship;
he establishes his lordship firmly in the common people.
All the Pavamāna [chants] of the Abhiṣecanīya are thirty-four-fold;
thirty-three are the deities;
they are reached and gratified by this for him.
Then this thirty-fourth [stoma]:
Prajāpati is the thirty-fourth;
he reaches Prajāpati.
This is a destruction of the stomas out of order when the stomas are unequal;
since the Pavamānas are equal,
thereby there is no destruction, thereby [they are] in order.
By the Agniṣṭoma he thrives through himself, he becomes meritorious through himself;
offspring are the ukthas,
cattle are the ukthas;
since there is the Ukthya,
[it is] for continuity.
Whoever departs from the consecration, the consecration does not fall to his lot;
whoever departs from the sāmans becomes worse off after pressing Soma;
since he does not depart from the sāmans of the divine kings,
thereby he does not depart from the consecrations.
These sāmans are the pṛṣṭhas;
since they undertake the pṛṣṭhas,
thereby he does not depart from the sāmans;
by the sāmans of the divine kings he thrives in this world, by those of human kings in yonder [world];
so by these he thrives in both worlds, in this one and in yonder.
They chant on verses containing [the word] 'ud';
what contains 'ud' is the form of the anuṣṭubh;
the Rājanya belongs to the anuṣṭubh;
therefore they chant on verses containing 'ud'.
The first is an anuṣṭubh, the last is an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is speech;
by speech they set forth, by speech they conclude.
The last anuṣṭubh belongs to Sūrya,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
The Bahiṣpavamāna of the Keśavapanīya is twenty-one-fold; the last [stoma] of the Abhiṣecanīya is twenty-one-fold;
the twenty-one-fold is the common people,
the seventeenfold is lordship;
he is consecrated from the midst of the common people;
therefore he is dear to the common people,
for he is consecrated from the midst of the common people.
When he makes him step out in those directions there, then he ascends to the heavenly world.
If he were not to descend again to this world, he would either go beyond the people or run wild.
Because this stoma is directed backward,
he descends again to this world.

4.5.1.m:
Vṛtra lay encompassing the waters.
Those which escaped beyond were the living, the sacrificial ones.
Vṛtra is a mountain; those which run having escaped are the living, the sacrificial ones.
When he draws from flowing [waters],
he draws from those very ones that are living and sacrificial.
He brings [the vessel] near against the current,
so that there be no failure.
He draws in the sun’s view;
he draws them with Indra indeed.
'These waters are rich in oblation,' [he says];
he draws them as rich in oblation.
They are to be drawn when the sun is visible;
he takes hold of the higher sacrificial rite.
He draws with an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is all metres;
with all metres he draws them.
This is the summit of the metres;
he causes him to reach the summit of his equals.
With four [verses] he draws, with three he sets down;
that makes seven.
The śakvarī has seven feet;
cattle belong to the Śakvarī;
he secures cattle.
Night enters into the waters when day has come;
therefore waters are dark by day.
Day [enters into] the waters at night;
therefore waters are bright at night.
When he draws by day,
he draws both day and night.
By day men perform the sacrifice, at night the gods;
therefore he seeks fame by nights.
Having concluded the sacrifice, men lie down.
The waters are indeed All-gods.
When they carry around the waters,
he delivers the sacrifice over to the waters.
[The sacrifice] is made continuous for his sacrifice.
What they omit in the sacrifice is a hole;
along that hole the rakṣases weave into the sacrifice.
The waters are slayers of rakṣases;
the rakṣases do not cross the waters.
When they carry around the waters,
[it is] so that the rakṣases do not descend upon [the sacrifice].
Those which he draws by day, they are the womb of those creatures that are eaters;
they are born along after them.
Those which he draws at night, they are the womb of those creatures that are to be eaten;
they are born along after them.
He generates both kinds of creatures: eaters and those to be eaten.
He sets [them] down in the Āgnīdhra [fire-shed];
for this is unconquered;
and also because the deities wait fasting here for that night.
The gods did not see the morrow of the sacrifice;
they saw this very morrow of the sacrifice, namely the Vasatīvarī [waters].
When they wait fasting having drawn the Vasatīvarī waters,
having seized the morrow of the sacrifice, they wait fasting.
And also, the waters are the All-gods;
having seized all deities, they wait fasting.
If the sun should set upon him when they are not drawn, they should be drawn from the jar of one who has established his fires and is a soma-sacrificer;
for they are drawn for him. They should hold fire above [the jar];
then he should draw;
he draws them as full of light.
Gold is in his hand;
then he draws;
gold is truth;
with truth he draws them.
A boon is the sacrificial gift;
with a boon he draws them.
Whatever wealth he might intend to give there, that should be given.

4.5.2.m:
He draws first with the pracaraṇī [ladle];
for this goes forward first.
The sap of Soma entered the waters;
Agni is all the deities;
the waters belong to the Viśvedevas;
the pressing-stones are the mouth of the sacrifice.
Impelled by Savitṛ, he goes after the sap of Soma [that is] of the waters with all the deities.
'Let Agni hear my call with the kindling stick,' he says;
he speaks to that very fire in which he offers.
'Let the waters hear,' he says;
he speaks to those very waters that he goes after.
'And the divine Dhiṣaṇā,' [he says];
speech is the divine Dhiṣaṇā;
he speaks to speech.
'Hear, O wise pressing-stones, the sacrifice now,' he says;
for the pressing-stones are the mouth of the sacrifice.
'Let the god Savitṛ hear my call,' he says,
for impulsion.
'Send the waters, O Hotṛ,' he says;
'impel the sacrifice,' is what he says by that.
'Forward with the sacred word (brahman),' he says;
with the sacred word he goes after the sacrifice.
He goes after [it] with the cup of the Maitrāvaruṇa;
for this goes forward first.
Moreover, Mitra and Varuṇa rule over the waters;
Mitra and Varuṇa are the mouth of the sacrifice;
the waters are the mouth of the sacrifice;
by the mouth of the sacrifice he goes after the mouth of the sacrifice.
Where the Adhvaryu would hear the Hotṛ reciting the morning recitation (prātaranuvāka), there he should draw the waters.
If he should not hear, he would become deaf,
for he is cut off from speech.
Then that he offers,
he draws them having become an oblation;
and also they are anointed with ghee.
Throwing in a blade of grass, he offers;
he offers right on what has fire, on what has an abode.
The Adhvaryu would become blind if he should offer on what has no abode.
Having made ghee a vajra, the gods slew Soma.
Indeed they besprinkle these [waters] with ghee;
therefore he washes away the oblation,
and also saying, 'Lest I burn the oblation.'
'Thou art furrow-born,' [saying this] he washes away the oblation.
'I draw you up for the non-exhaustion of the ocean,' he brings [it] near;
for the non-exhaustion of the waters.
The sap of Soma entered the waters;
the sap of the waters [entered] Soma.
Apāṃ Napāt rules over the sap of the waters [and] of Soma;
he begged the sap of the waters [and] of Soma away from both of them with an oblation as their share.
They say: 'Both day and night are to be taken in the Vasatīvarī [waters];
he draws one on the previous day, the other before the rising of the sun;
thus both day and night are drawn for him in the Vasatīvarī [waters].'
He draws one on the previous day, the other before the rising of the sun;
he has made both of them drawn on the same day.
Enemies indeed are drawn: those in the cup of the Maitrāvaruṇa and those which are the Nigrābhyā [waters];
he anoints both of them together above the pit (cātvāla);
he makes agreement for them;
he gives a friend to them.
They are drawn out from the pit;
this is the womb of the waters;
they are drawn out from their own womb,
so that there be no spilling.
'Hast thou obtained the waters, O Adhvaryu?' [one asks];
'Hast thou found the sacrifice?' is what he says by that.
'And they have bowed down,'
'and behold this one!'
'This [sacrifice] has succeeded for me,' is what he says by that.
If [the sacrifice] is an Agniṣṭoma, he offers;
they do not go beyond Agni by the stotra nor by the śastra of the Agniṣṭoma;
he connects the Agniṣṭoma sacrificial rite continuously.
If it is an Ukthya, he anoints the enclosing-stick;
the enclosing-sticks are beyond Agni;
the ukthas are beyond the enclosing-sticks;
he connects the ukthas sacrificial rite continuously.
If it is an Atirātra, uttering this very yajus, he should enter the Havirdhāna shed;
the Havirdhāna shed is beyond the enclosing-sticks;
the night is beyond the ukthas;
he connects the night sacrificial rite continuously.
The mind is a thief;
speech tells lies.
Now, with what are the somas drawn, with what are they offered? If one should ask,
gold is truth and cosmic order (ṛta);
by truth, by cosmic order they are drawn; by cosmic order they are offered.

4.5.3.m:
The sacrifice is brought forward for the gods, for humans, [and] for the heavenly world.
When [he says], 'For your heart,'
thereby [it is] for the gods.
When [he says], 'For your mind,'
thereby [it is] for humans.
When he says,
'For heaven you, for the sun you,'
thereby he makes the sacrifice ascend after him to the heavenly world.
The sacrifice is indeed severed from him for whose sake it is brought forward;
therefore he makes him ascend after it to the heavenly world.
When he says,
'Extend the hotrā among the gods in heaven,'
the sacrifice is continuous through the hotrās;
by means of the hotrās he continues the sacrifice.
'Recite for the gods who go early in the morning,' [he says];
the meters are the gods who go early in the morning;
to the meters he thereby speaks the direction to recite.
Before speech speaks forth, he recites the morning litany (prātaranuvāka);
he grasps speech for the sacrificer alone.
The sacrificer does not wash his hands at that time;
when he recites as the first [verse] one containing the word 'water' (apsumatī),
that is the morning washing of the sacrificer.
Where they carry the waters around over there, he then handed over the sacrifice to the waters;
when he recites as the first [verse] one containing the word 'water',
he thereby takes hold of the sacrifice again from the waters.
The morning litany belongs to all the gods;
the waters are indeed all the gods;
when he recites as the first [verse] one containing the word 'water',
he thereby obtains all the deities, he completely reaches all.
Just as the sāmidhenī verses, so is the morning litany;
having placed the oblation, they recite the sāmidhenī verses;
the oblation at that time is Soma;
therefore having brought down Soma, he recites.
He brings about both the Gāyatrī and the Jagatī;
those are two meters;
he brings them about into one meter, the Bṛhatī.
And the Triṣṭubh and the Kakubh;
those are two meters;
he brings them about into one meter, the Bṛhatī.
And the Anuṣṭubh and the Paṅkti;
those are six meters;
he brings them about into one meter, the Bṛhatī.
He should make the meters have an Anuṣṭubh opening for one whose sacrifice has been disrupted;
the Anuṣṭubh is speech;
with speech he heals him.
He should recite the Triṣṭubh and the Jagatī evenly for one desiring cattle;
the Jagatī is cattle;
he becomes possessed of cattle.
He recites all the meters;
the meters are Prajāpati;
he obtains Prajāpati.
The morning litany is this hymn (uktha) of Prajāpati;
he should recite at dawn before the rising of the sun;
for this is the world of Prajāpati.
The morning litany is a means of purification;
by this he purifies the sacrificer.
He should recite one hundred [verses] for one desiring life;
man is of a hundred years of life, of a hundredfold vigor;
he obtains life and vigor.
He should recite seven hundred and twenty [verses] for one desiring cattle;
so many are the days and nights of the year;
by means of the year he wins cattle for him.
He should recite one thousand for one desiring heaven;
as far as a thousand [steps], so far reached is that world;
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
He recites an unlimited number [of verses];
[it is] for the winning of the unlimited.
He concludes with a Paṅkti;
he takes hold of the higher sacrificial rite.

4.5.4.m:
These are the foot-washings of the sacrifice.
The Agnīdh belongs to the males;
the Neṣṭṛ belongs to the females.
When the Agnīdh sits upon the lap of the Neṣṭṛ,
they two indeed unite as a pair.
When at that time she rolls the waters forward,
in that very pair he places seed.
The wife rolls [them] forward;
for from the wife offspring are born.
She rolls [them] forward along her thigh;
for offspring are born along the thigh.
She rolls [them] forward along her right thigh;
for offspring are born along the right thigh.
She rolls [them] forward on the inside;
for offspring are born from the inside.
If she were to roll [them] forward southward, she would sweep them away into the world of the ancestors.
Now when she rolls them forward northward,
she brings them forth into the world of humans.
The Udgātṛ belongs to Prajāpati;
being looked at [by the wife], the Udgātṛ takes the seed of the wife.
When she says,
'Pleasing in your sight, may I deposit all seed,'
thus indeed he does not take [it].
At the twelfth stotra she rolls [them] forward;
twelve months are the year;
twelve are the stotras in the Agniṣṭoma;
waters are appeasement.
When at that time she rolls the waters forward,
he simply appeases [it].
'At the impulse of the god Savitṛ,' [he says];
impelled by Savitṛ he takes it.
'With the arms of the Aśvins,' [he says];
the Aśvins are the two Adhvaryus of the gods.
'With the hands of Pūṣan,' [he says];
[he takes it] through the deities indeed.
'You are a pressing-stone,' [he says];
for this [stone] offers Soma to the gods.
'Sacrifice-maker for the gods,' [he says];
for this [stone] makes the sacrifice for the gods.
'For Indra, you best pressed, sweet, rich in milk,' [he says];
for the sacrifice belongs to Indra.
Indeed, at this time the other deities follow Indra.
'For Indra accompanied by the Vasus, accompanied by the Rudras,' [he says];
for the Vasus and the Rudras follow him at this time.
'For Indra accompanied by the Ādityas,' [he says];
for the Ādityas follow him at this time.
'For Agni who gives increase of wealth,' [he says];
Agni is all the deities;
for these deities follow him at this time.
'For Viṣṇu, you,' [he says];
for Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
The Upāṃśusavana [pressing-stone] is the out-breathing;
the Soma shoots are offspring;
he places the out-breathing in offspring.
'Invigorating are you, overcoming Vṛtra,' [he says];
this is the Soma-drink of the waters.
Viśvāmitra knew this Soma-drink of the waters;
to him the rivers yielded milking.
This is the Soma-drink of the waters;
he enriches the waters with the Soma-drink.
Whoever knows the Soma-drink of the waters suffers no misfortune among them; he performs valor among them.
'The light of yours, O Soma, which is in heaven,' [he says];
Soma is prize-wealth;
a third of it is the moon;
that which purifies itself here is a third;
that with which they sacrifice is a third.
By means of that one [above] he upholds the embryos; by means of this one he extends the life-breaths;
that which purifies itself here, by means of it he upholds food;
[that] with which they sacrifice—of that prize-wealth indeed, truth is the prize-winner,
food is the prize-winner,
the sacrificial gift is the prize-winner.
Having gathered these bodies of his, they swell him when he has become whole with body.
The pressing-stone is a thunderbolt;
the two pressing-boards are jaws;
something cruel, as it were, is done here in the sacrifice.
When he says,
'O praised Dhiṣaṇās, be praised,'
he simply appeases [it].

4.5.5.m:
With the Vasatīvarī [waters] they swell the Soma;
for Soma is not swelled by Soma;
for Soma is swelled by waters.
With the Nigrābhyā [waters they swell] the Upāṃśu [cup];
for from this the other Somas are drawn.
The Upāṃśu is breath;
the other cups are the limbs.
If they were to swell [it] from elsewhere, they would sever the sacrifice from the breath.
He presses eight times in the morning.
The gāyatrī has eight syllables;
they thereby stretch out the gāyatrī at the morning pressing.
The gods and the Asuras contended.
The gods saw the sacrifice as one to be established in the Upāṃśu;
they established it in the Upāṃśu.
Therefore the sacrifice is to be established in the Upāṃśu.
He presses eight times in the morning;
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
he obtains the gāyatrī.
When eleven,
thereby [he obtains] the triṣṭubh.
When twelve,
thereby [he obtains] the jagatī.
Three times he draws out;
three indeed are the pressings;
he obtains the pressings.
Thus indeed they established the sacrifice in the Upāṃśu.
With that established, uninjured, existing [sacrifice] they proceed.
Three times he draws out;
for these breaths are three: prāṇa, apāna, [and] vyāna.
Moreover, these worlds are three;
he obtains these worlds.
Yonder [world] is the Upāṃśu;
the intermediate space is the Antaryāma;
this [earth] is the Upayāma.
When [cups] held by an Upayāma are drawn,
they are drawn by this [earth].
Therefore they are unspilled;
for they are drawn by this [earth].
When they are drawn with an earthen pot,
they are drawn by this [earth];
for this [pot] is made of it.
Therefore they are unspilled;
for they are drawn by this [earth].
When they are drawn with a wooden [vessel],
they are drawn by this [earth];
for this [earth] is the womb of trees.
Therefore they are unspilled;
for they are drawn by this [earth].
'This is your womb,' he says, setting it down;
this [earth] is the womb of Soma;
from this Soma is born.
He places it in its own womb.
This [earth] is the vessel of the gods.
Therefore whoever knows thus obtains the vessel of one who is wealthier.
But he should not investigate a Brahmin at the drinking vessel who would be worthy of the vessel.
It is drawn not held by an Upayāma;
for offspring subsist on the breath not held by an Upayāma.
It is drawn unpurified by a strainer;
for offspring subsist on the breath unpurified by a strainer.
It is offered unseated;
for offspring subsist on the breath unseated.
The other Somas are purified by a strainer;
now this Upāṃśu is purified by speech.
Therefore he says, 'Purify yourself for the lord of speech!';
for it is purified by speech.
He purifies it with Soma-stalks;
the stalks are the breaths;
he purifies it with the breaths.
He purifies it with two by two;
for these breaths are two by two.
'A god, you are the strainer of the gods,' [he says],
when indeed he purifies Soma with Soma.
'You are prepared for oneself,' [he says];
he has made the breath his own.
'May the mind reach you,' [he says];
he obtains the breath with the mind.
'Svāhā to you, well-being to the sun,' [he says];
for all Somas are offered having the sun as their deity.
Moreover, here indeed are the dear bodies of the gods;
he secures them.
'To you for the gods who drink the rays,' [he says];
for the gods who drink the rays are the rays [of the sun];
the enclosing sticks are the rays;
yonder sun is the Āhavanīya [fire];
he pleases them.
Standing upright, he offers;
for standing upright he is more vigorous.
For him whom he hates, he should offer standing obliquely;
he crushes his breaths;
he becomes tottering.
If he performs sorcery, he should stand until he faints;
Agni, hastening toward the oblation, immediately follows.
If he should desire, 'May Parjanya rain,' having wiped [the cup] around, he should wipe upward;
he rains from yonder for the plants;
he brings the plants nearer to the rain.
If he should desire, 'May Parjanya rain,' having wiped around, he should wipe downward;
with Vaiśvānara he strikes down the rain.
It is not to be covered;
if he were to cover it, he would cover his breath, [and] he would be liable to die.
For him whom he hates, he should cover it;
he covers his breath.
If he performs sorcery, he should cover it [saying],
'Here I cover the breath of so-and-so, son of so-and-so';
he covers his breath.
'Here I set [this] down in the breath of so-and-so, son of so-and-so,' [so] performing sorcery he should set it down;
he sets it down in his breath;
he becomes liable to die.
'For breath, you,' he should set it down when not performing sorcery.

4.5.7.m:
The expounders of the Veda say:
'What is done in the sacrifice because of which some animals take with the hand, others with the mouth?'
Because the Upāṃśu is drawn with the hand,
therefore the monkey, man, and the elephant—these take with the hand.
The Vāyavya is the mouth.
Because the other cups are drawn in the Vāyavya,
therefore the other animals take with the mouth.
The expounders of the Veda say:
'What is done in the sacrifice because of which newborn animals stand firmly on the same day, but men [only] in a year?'
The other animals follow the Upāṃśu and Antaryāma;
man, however, is commensurate with the sacrifice.
Upāṃśu and Antaryāma are in-breath and out-breath.
Because these two cups are offered without being set down,
therefore newborn animals stand firmly on the same day.
The Agniṣṭoma is the year;
twelve months are the year.
Because these other cups are set down,
therefore man when born stands firmly in a year.
When [he says], 'What uncheatable name of yours is watchful, O Soma...',
this indeed is the Soma-drink of Soma.
He thereby enriches Soma with the Soma-drink.
He who knows the Soma-drink of Soma is not severed from the Soma-drink.
Soma desired these quarters:
east, west, downward, [and] north.
He brings it together with these quarters;
he enriches it with these;
he causes it to attain desire.
He goes to his desire;
women become desirous of him.
Whomever he should desire, upon her he should then meditate in mind;
she desires him.
'O mother, remember,' [he says];
'Remember' is what he thereby says.
'May I find with the stranger,' [he says];
'Find together with this one' is what he thereby says.
There were four teats of Pṛśni;
from there she milked for the gods with three.
One was tied round with points of kuśa grass.
Indra alone saw it;
with it she milked for Indra alone.
That indeed is his Kauśika nature,
when he says, 'O Brahmin, like the Kauśikas.'
Four times he approaches the Nigrābha;
there are four milks;
whatever milk there is, that he obtains.
Three times he gathers [it];
therefore there are three shares in this [cow]: in the morning, at midday, in the evening.
Four times he approaches the Nigrābha, three times he gathers [it];
that is seven.
The śakvarī has seven feet;
animals are connected with the Śakvarī;
he secures animals.
Nine times he approaches the Nigrābha, three times he gathers [it];
that is twelve.
Twelve months are the year;
twelve are the chants in the Agniṣṭoma;
this is the measure of the sacrifice.
Svarbhānu the Asura pierced the sun with darkness;
the gods struck away its darkness.
The first darkness that they struck away, that became a black sheep;
the second, that [became] a reddish one;
the third, that [became] a white one.
It was freed in its own form.
Because the strainer is white, from it the Śukra [cup] is drawn.
This is the sacrificer's strainer;
he purifies the sacrificer with it.
It should be unspun [by others];
he made it from his own self.
What is spun elsewhere is not a strainer.
That belongs to the sacrificer.

4.5.9.m:
The gods indeed held a sacrificial session at Kurukṣetra: Agni, Makha, Vāyu, Indra.
They said:
'Whichever of us shall prosper first, that shall belong to us together.'
Makha indeed among them prospered.
He desired it exclusively for himself;
he did not share it.
They wished to take it from him by force.
From here he produced three [arrows];
from here, a bow.
That is the origin of the three [arrows] and of the bow.
Having strung [the bow], he stepped back; they did not dare approach him.
He stood resting against the bow-end.
Indra said to the ants:
'Gnaw through this bowstring.'
They said:
'When she (the earth) is trampled upon, we will not be able to live.
Let there be a share for us.'
He said:
'Live off the sap of this [earth].'
Therefore they throw up moist [earth] from the dry,
for they live off her sap.
They indeed gnawed through the bowstring.
The bow-end of it, springing up, cut off his head.
He became the Samrāj.
Then the rest they divided into three parts: Agni [took] the front half, Indra the middle, Vāyu the hind half.
Therefore the morning pressing belongs to Agni, the midday pressing to Indra, the third pressing to the All-gods,
for Vāyu is the All-gods.
The Upāṃśu and Antaryāma [cups] are in-breath and out-breath;
the Upāṃśusavana is diffused breath;
the Aindravāyava is speech;
the Maitrāvaruṇa is skill and resolve;
the Āśvina is hearing;
the Śukra and Manthin are the two eyes;
the Āgrayaṇa is the self;
the Ukthyas are the limbs;
the Dhruva is life-span;
the two Ṛtupātras are the breasts;
the droṇakalaśa is the head;
the two kalasas are the flanks;
the two adhiṣavaṇa [boards] are the jaws;
the adhiṣavaṇa [skin] is the tongue;
the pressing-stones are the teeth;
the enclosing-sticks are the collarbones;
the uttaravedi is the nose;
the sacrificial post is the crest of hair;
the havirdhāna is the head;
the Āgnīdhra is the back;
the sadas is the belly;
what is within the belly, that is the dhiṣṇyas; the two hands
and the Āgnīdhra and Mārjālīya are the two heels;
the Gārhapatya is the firm standing; the altar [is the hips].
This Prajāpati indeed is embodied in the vessels;
he truly knows him, who knows thus.

4.6.1.m:
Nine of these cups are drawn in the morning.
These nine vital breaths, indeed, are drawn.
The Āśvina is drawn as the tenth.
The out-breathing (vyāna), indeed, is drawn [in this];
the navel, indeed, is drawn as the tenth.
It is offered third,
for this breath is the third,
for the continuity of the vital breaths.
All these cups possess a chant (stotra), possess a recitation (śastra), and possess a primary cause (nidāna).
He comes to possess a primary cause who knows thus.
The Hotṛ, indeed, sacrificed today in the morning to the Upāṃśu;
thereby these [cups] possess Ukthas.
Moreover, what silent recitation (tūṣṇīṃśaṃsa) the Hotṛ recites, thereby indeed these [cups] possess Ukthas.
Nine of these cups are drawn in the morning; with nine [verses] they chant in the Bahiṣpavamāna;
thereby these possess sāmans.
With the hiṃkāra the Āśvina possesses a sāman;
with the Aindravāyava [chant] the Aindravāyava [possesses a sāman];
with the Maitrāvaruṇa [chant] the Maitrāvaruṇa;
with the Aindra [chant] the Śukra and Manthin;
with the Vaiśvadeva [chant] the Āgrayaṇa.
For the recitation (uktha) the Ukthya [cup] is drawn;
for the recitation the Dhruva [cup];
for continuity the Aindrāgna [cup];
for continuity the Marutvatīya [cups].
The Adhvaryu offers, the Hotṛ pronounces the vaṣaṭ-call;
thereby the two bestow the vital breaths.
With the mantra by which the Adhvaryu hands it over, with that the Hotṛ receives it,
for the guarding of the vital breaths.
Prajāpati himself, indeed, was the Hotṛ;
he became exhausted.
The gods ran to him with the [cups] for two deities.
The vital breaths, indeed, are the [cups] for two deities.
When the Adhvaryu, having offered the cups for two deities, quickly runs to the Hotṛ,
he thereby refreshes the Hotṛ with the vital breaths.
At every vaṣaṭ-call the Hotṛ reaches the end of his breath.
The vital breaths, indeed, are the [cups] for two deities.
When the Adhvaryu, having offered the cups for two deities, quickly runs to the Hotṛ,
he thereby refreshes the Hotṛ with the vital breaths.
The vital breaths, indeed, are the [cups] for two deities;
cattle are the iḍā.
If, without consuming the cups for two deities, he were to invoke the iḍā, he would cut off the vital breaths by means of cattle.
Now when, having consumed the cups for two deities, he invokes the iḍā,
having placed the vital breaths in himself, he then invokes cattle.
Power (indriya), indeed, is Soma;
cattle are power.
When he consumes the Soma,
he places power in himself.
He consumes the Āśvina turning it round from all sides;
therefore one hears in all directions.

4.7.5.m:
By means of the sacrifice, indeed, the gods went to the heavenly world.
They thought:
“By this [path], indeed, others will ascend after us to the world.”
They saw the metres;
they interlaced them,
so that the heavenly world would not be discerned.
In that he interlaces the metres,
the sacrificer thereby confounds the world and the metres of the rival,
so that the heavenly world would not be discerned.
Among the gods, indeed, were some metres, among the Asuras others.
Smaller ones were among the gods, larger ones among the Asuras.
Those gods with the smaller metres wrested away the larger metres of the Asuras.
In that he recites the larger metres differently with the smaller metres,
the sacrificer thereby wrests away the world and the metres of the rival;
he deprives him of world and metres.
He recites all the metres into the Anuṣṭubh.
The Anuṣṭubh, indeed, is speech.
Man is of the nature of the Anuṣṭubh;
therefore man speaks all speech.
The metres, indeed, are cattle;
speech is the Anuṣṭubh;
man is of the nature of the Anuṣṭubh.
In that, having recited all the metres, he recites the Anuṣṭubh last, therefore man above consumes the speechless cattle.
He recites all the metres together;
Prajāpati, indeed, is the metres;
he attains Prajāpati.
Six syllables are redundant from the stotra;
six, indeed, are the seasons;
he firmly establishes himself in the seasons.
From them four return back to the śastra;
by that he secures four-footed cattle.
Then two only are left over;
two, truth and falsehood, surpass the Virāj;
he attains the Virāj.

4.7.6.m:
Prajāpati, indeed, distributed the sacrifices for the gods.
He thought:
“I have excluded myself.”
He tore out their powers and virtues again;
that became the Ṣoḍaśin.
Now at that time Indra was the lowest, the slackest among the gods.
To him he gave this Ṣoḍaśin.
By means of it Indra prospered;
thence the gods prospered, the Asuras were defeated.
Therefore one who, knowing thus, draws this Ṣoḍaśin cup prospers himself, [and] his rival is defeated.
Prajāpati, indeed, distributed bodies for the gods.
The one of them that had bay horses, that one he left for himself out of love.
When the puroruc of the cup contains [the word] ‘bay horses’,
that very dear body of his that has bay horses he attains.
They say: “There is no sacrifice named Ṣoḍaśin.”
Then why [is it called] Ṣoḍaśin?
Because it is made complete by both stotra and śastra, therefore [it is called] Ṣoḍaśin.
By means of the sacrifice, indeed, the gods went to the heavenly world.
It did not suffice for them.
Onto it, indeed, they fastened this Ṣoḍaśin;
by means of it they reached yonder world.
Therefore, indeed, the Ṣoḍaśin is drawn for reaching yonder world, for the attainment of yonder world.
At an Agniṣṭoma one should draw it for a nobleman;
unappeased, the thunderbolt kindles him for prosperity.
At an Atirātra for a Brahmin;
the Ṣoḍaśin is indeed a thunderbolt, capable of injuring the sacrificer when unappeased;
the night, indeed, appeases the thunderbolt.
It should be drawn at the morning pressing;
the morning pressing is brilliance;
he constructs the thunderbolt from brilliance itself.
It should be drawn at the midday pressing;
the midday pressing is force;
he constructs the thunderbolt from force itself.
It should be drawn at the third pressing;
the third pressing is cattle;
he constructs the thunderbolt from cattle themselves.
If he were to draw it at the morning pressing, the thunderbolt would be left over beyond the two later pressings.
If at the midday pressing, the thunderbolt would strike down in the middle.
It should be drawn at the third pressing;
thus he draws it for all the pressings, nor are the two earlier pressings brought to ruin.
It should be made fifteen-versed;
the fifteen-versed is indeed a thunderbolt;
he places the thunderbolt in him;
thereby he goes to conquest [and] prosperity.
It should be made sixteen-versed;
the sacrificer is fifteen-versed, the thunderbolt is sixteen-versed;
he places the thunderbolt in him;
it kindles him for prosperity, for supremacy.
It should be made seventeen-versed;
Prajāpati is seventeen-versed;
the Ṣoḍaśin belongs to Prajāpati;
he places him in his own womb.
It should be made twenty-one-versed;
the Anuṣṭubh has twenty-one domains;
the Ṣoḍaśin is of the nature of the Anuṣṭubh;
this is its closest abode;
therefore it should be made twenty-one-versed.
On the middle day of a three-day rite it should be drawn;
the middle day of a three-day rite is slack;
[it is drawn] for the firmness of the day, for unslackness.
On the fourth day of a four-day rite it should be drawn;
the fourth day of a four-day rite is slack;
[it is drawn] for the firmness of the day, for unslackness.
On every fourth day of an Ahīna rite it should be drawn;
every fourth day of an Ahīna rite is slack;
[it is drawn] for the firmness of the day, for unslackness.
It is not suitable in a two-day rite;
these two metres are indeed the Gāyatrī and the Triṣṭubh;
he would draw it without a metre, without an abode; the sacrificer would become destitute of an abode.
On the latter day of a two-day rite it should be drawn;
it extends beyond into the night as its abode.

4.7.8.m:
A black-headed [victim] belongs to Agni;
a ewe to Sarasvatī;
a brown one to Soma;
a dark one to Pūṣan;
a white-backed one to Bṛhaspati;
a reddish-brown one to the Viśve Devas;
a ram to Indra;
a spotted one to the Maruts;
a piebald one to Indra and Agni;
a white-bellied one to Savitṛ;
a gelded ram to Varuṇa.
Prajāpati, having created creatures, felt emptied.
He saw this set of eleven [victims];
with it he replenished himself.
The sacrifice, indeed, is Prajāpati.
Therefore one who, knowing thus, ties up this set of eleven [victims] replenishes whatever of himself is deficient.
Prajāpati, having created creatures, felt emptied.
He saw these powers and virtues;
he bound them together in pairs onto himself.
The body belongs to Agni;
speech to Sarasvatī;
power to Soma.
Speech and power he bound together onto his body.
Nourishment belongs to Pūṣan;
the sacred lore to Bṛhaspati.
Nourishment and the sacred lore he bound together onto himself.
Strength belongs to the Viśve Devas;
virility to Indra.
Strength and virility he bound together onto himself.
Might belongs to the Maruts;
force to Indra and Agni.
Might and force he bound together onto himself.
The one for Savitṛ is for impulsion;
the one for Varuṇa is for freedom from Varuṇa.
All are tied after the one for Agni.
Agni, indeed, is all the deities;
they are tied, indeed, after all the deities.
The one for Sarasvatī is for pairing.
Now, as to why [there is] one for Soma:
Soma, indeed, is the placer of semen;
he places semen in him.
Now, as to why [there is] one for Pūṣan:
Pūṣan, indeed, is procreation;
[it is] for procreation.
The sacred lore is Bṛhaspati;
these creatures belong to the Viśve Devas.
He pushed the sacred lore ahead of these creatures;
moreover, he makes these creatures compliant with the sacred lore.
The Maruts are the peasantry;
he thereby harnesses the peasantry to the ruling power;
moreover, he makes the peasantry compliant with the ruling power;
by might he encompasses the peasantry for the ruling power.
The one for Agni is seized;
the one for Indra is seized;
then this one for Indra and Agni is seized:
he has made the sacred lore and the ruling power yoke-fellows.
Since Agni and Indra
are the most possessing of shares among the deities,
therefore the Brahmin and the king are the most possessing of shares among men.
The one for Savitṛ is for impulsion;
the one for Varuṇa is for freedom from Varuṇa.
Varuṇa, indeed, is the ocean;
the south is the ocean.
In that the one for Varuṇa is seized on the southern half,
[it is] for the sacrificer's freedom from Varuṇa.
Concerning this, indeed, this [verse] has been recited:
“You are a true god, O Varuṇa, for whom the seven rivers
flow down into your palate like a hollow tube.”
Thus [it says].
